course: dictionary
lesson: 15344
title: Solely: only, and nothing else
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Solely» is a word that means only. When you meet it, ask yourself: is this one thing on its own, with nobody or nothing else involved? If it is, solely fits.
explain: «The pool is solely for members.» Only members may swim there. Nobody else is included, so the word limits who can use the pool.
explain: «You are solely responsible for your bag.» Responsible on your own, not shared with anyone. Solely can also mean completely or entirely, but most often it means only.
fact: Solely means only, or not involving anyone or anything else.
fact: Solely can also mean completely or entirely.
fact: «The pool is solely for members» means members only, and nobody else.
fact: In maths, «solely red counters» means you count only the red ones.
fact: Solely often comes before words like for, because and responsible: solely responsible, solely because.
exercise: «The pool is solely for members.» What does solely mean here? | Together with everyone | Quickly | Loudly | *Only
exercise: «Mia counted solely the red counters in the bag.» What did Mia count? | *Only the red ones | The blue and red ones | Some green ones | All the counters in the bag
exercise: «She went solely because her friend asked her.» What was the only reason she went? | *Only because her friend asked her | Because the teacher said so | To get some sweets | To find her lost hat
test: «You are solely responsible for your library book.» What does solely tell you? | *You alone must look after it | The book is lost | Everyone shares the book | The library is closed
test: Which word means the same as solely in «the pool is solely for members»? | Lonely | *Only | Slowly | Sadly
exam: Which sentence means that only Sam is allowed to drive the car? | Sam and Ali can both drive the car. | Sam drives the car quickly and carefully. | *Sam is solely allowed to drive the car. | The car is solely red, so Sam drives it.
